#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/id #uuid "037b7db9-131c-5a08-aa84-d7552a903e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-21726" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 9y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ 9-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$\\left( \\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1fa1647-1acb-50bd-95e7-26ed0c495bd4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/id #uuid "47c1e08d-d5b4-5f1e-b225-d1336645898f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21727" :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=-0.4x+0.4xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=y-4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=-0.4+0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4xy)=-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4xy)=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/e5/e526dd9bda292c2a25920b8248820f3ed88c0bc4c4a55e3ba3f2db925af486d8.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/cc/cc6e0f2742f419fc8f2c09bf69c97f72d7e7f1ca0d98c3845996e216849f4316.png) to get\n\n![](images/bc/bcc787d9cceae4a7280c6af84c0d7bf95c15dfc90cf9e3bdedf2a52362c1b225.png)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-4)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm 0.2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a stable center.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "98a0cd06-80a8-514c-bc89-c739d8502fda" :question/math-academy-id "e-21728" :question/problem "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-10x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=21y-7xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's describe the system:\n\n- The trajectory circles around the nonzero equilibrium.\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey decreases and the number of predators increases."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/id #uuid "2266e14f-c662-5844-bfb4-3b4259f9232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21734" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-8x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor the $x'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\nx' = -8x + 2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the predator. Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-8x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2xy$ term).\n\nFor the $y'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\ny' = 4y - xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the prey. Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9779cf2-ecbc-5e94-a5ec-29b826455265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164615} {:answer/id #uuid "c28692c9-a912-5b23-9e69-3613b0f091d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164616}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cb8319-193c-5e8a-97b2-dd5956bf2d33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "443be137-9bc1-5f40-8ea1-24afed97d526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164618} {:answer/id #uuid "5b38a944-fffa-50a4-b4b4-37235f5f0802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0b065747-9d5b-53aa-bedd-dc536d99e671" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164617}] :question/id #uuid "767ef314-be44-5541-862e-af1885bb844d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334774" :question/problem "${x^{′} =-4x + 2xy; y^{′} = 6y - 2xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$. Select the correct options in the following statement.\nTerm $y^{′}$ in the second equation represents the\n{{field-1}}\nof the\n{{field-2}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9479d2-cbfb-5345-96fe-5db52a2bfdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370172} {:answer/id #uuid "928a10d4-85a1-58a7-996c-2b7a024d0c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9479d2-cbfb-5345-96fe-5db52a2bfdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370172} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e658e15-e093-5ab8-b271-2c0a1b0834fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1973650f-7466-5fac-be98-b91c9a6bb3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370175} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f7a61f-1667-541b-b5eb-eab40fabf669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f7a61f-1667-541b-b5eb-eab40fabf669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370176} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e763f25-55a8-5e05-b176-9a86fa5cdf1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ccaa6cb-56f1-5809-80e7-633a3a4dc02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334985" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2.5x-0.5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+0.8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2.5x-0.5xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $2.5x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-0.5xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9a31435d-8fe2-5feb-858b-299a54090b86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164695} {:answer-field/id #uuid "24cfd6f5-d55a-54c1-8618-6fd8923c6333" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fab5b32-10a1-5a46-94ca-9eb01b58c24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fab5b32-10a1-5a46-94ca-9eb01b58c24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d434ee-ec78-51ed-8e2a-b9f813737294" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1a7389-5d16-5f46-9650-c5051a6920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb1a7389-5d16-5f46-9650-c5051a6920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370180} :answer-field/id #uuid "874b57c5-019e-572e-93ae-81d874074f37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0f816f5-6732-572e-beb4-ba284ca82c1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334992" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy & =0 \\\\ x(3-2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ -4+5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895380dc-30e3-5fd2-bca3-c6f6c57018ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895380dc-30e3-5fd2-bca3-c6f6c57018ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370193} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04708cb-1115-59d0-a2c4-ebbfdb85401a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc10c4dd-0f2c-55a3-bde8-eae31ae0d2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc10c4dd-0f2c-55a3-bde8-eae31ae0d2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370195} :answer-field/id #uuid "5acf4717-4ac3-5126-9b5d-7a7393e636d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecc92fa2-9bc0-55ee-9c55-3ca595f577e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335000" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-5x+4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy=0 \\\\ 9y-2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy & =0 \\\\ x(-5+4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{5}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "401db806-372a-5fd6-8992-621c15114775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "401db806-372a-5fd6-8992-621c15114775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370198} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51c4e9d-4fc3-5b94-9fbb-2f1729282196" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370197} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1667-541b-5035-a21b-fbdba15ef778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1667-541b-5035-a21b-fbdba15ef778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370200} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe78aeb-3059-5e8a-9a99-9dcbe521ca11" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0c7e73b-2cb7-5aa8-bc2d-519f7e72ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-335006" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy=0 \\\\ 7y-3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ 7-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a2af56-80f8-51d0-81e6-386c6d2e4869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370281} {:answer/id #uuid "42f15a47-847b-52f6-a792-7f19e2f65c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f15a47-847b-52f6-a792-7f19e2f65c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370282} :answer-field/id #uuid "fccf4520-0225-5f46-879f-c5c1d6a6f1be" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fff24be-bf5e-59e2-8ea3-c